    <div class="hero-title" style="font-size:2.4rem">How TQM Was Built</div>
    <div class="hero-tagline" style="font-size:1.05rem">From the TRM predecessor to the v1.0 release — a narrative, not a derivation.</div>
</div>

<div class="page-section mt-4">
    <div class="prose">
        <p class="lead">
            THE Q-MODEL did not arrive as a finished axiomatic system. It was assembled, and repeatedly
            stripped down, over a sequence of experiments, audits, and hostile reviews. This page tells that
            story in order.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">1.</span>The predecessor: TRM</h2>
        <p>
            TQM's immediate predecessor was the <em>Temporal Resonance Model</em> (TRM), a framework built on
            phase-lattice machinery. TRM had already assembled a striking collection of pieces: a Time Field
            that identified gravity with the phase gradient of a temporal field; a Radial Acceleration
            Relation; Frame Dragging; a Memory Channel; a Theta Chain; an <em>m = 3</em> Closure mechanism; a
            Quantum Engine; a tired-light Temporal Drift; and a Unified Action roadmap.
        </p>
        <p>
            TRM supplied the conceptual vocabulary — a temporal field, oscillation, phase, resonance — on which
            the later theory would be rebuilt. But TRM was a collection of ideas, not yet a theory: it had never
            been systematically audited against a hostile standard.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">2.</span>Problems found: the legacy audit</h2>
        <p>
            The decisive turn was the <em>TRM Legacy Final Classification</em>, a systematic audit that asked
            what, if anything, of the older framework should migrate into a new one. The answer was blunt: TRM
            carried <strong>zero candidate physics</strong> — no module supplied a genuinely new testable
            observable.
        </p>
        <p>
            Of the nine modules, three were absorbed (the Time Field became phase-gradient gravity; the RAR
            became the zero-parameter relation g† = cH0/2π; Frame Dragging was recognized as GR
            gravitomagnetism). Two were rejected outright: the tired-light Temporal Drift was falsified, and the
            Quantum Engine proved non-unitary. Three survived only as candidate mathematics. The residue of
            value was methodological.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">3.</span>ResearchX: alternative foundations</h2>
        <p>
            A parallel track, ResearchX, attacked the foundations from the side. It found eleven hidden
            assumptions — the static graph chief among them — and then tested them one by one. Dynamic graphs
            produced no open-ended innovation. Nonlinearity broke the eigenmode picture, showing that the early
            "quantum" results were linear-algebra artifacts.
        </p>
        <p>
            Out of this stripping emerged the two deep results that anchor the theory: <em>reversibility</em>
            plus <em>self-consistency</em> is the minimally sufficient pair whose intersection is unitary quantum
            mechanics, and quantum reality is the unique global maximum of finite complexity — a necessity, not
            an anthropic accident.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">4.</span>The structure/content split</h2>
        <p>
            The organizing principle of TQM was not chosen in advance; it was discovered. An audit asked a
            blunt question: given the primitives, what can actually be <em>computed</em>, and what merely
            <em>is</em>? The answer divided cleanly: everything derivable was form, symmetry, or topology;
            everything that resisted derivation was a dimensionless pure number.
        </p>
        <blockquote>Structure is derivable. Content is realized.</blockquote>
        <p>
            Since no combination of the dimensionful triple (ℓ, τ, ℏ) can produce a dimensionless number, the
            couplings cannot be assembled from the primitives. That turned a limitation into a principle, and
            produced the three-category taxonomy:
            <span class="badge badge-derived">DERIVED</span>
            <span class="badge badge-real">REAL-UNDERIVED</span>
            <span class="badge badge-drawn">DRAWN</span>.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">5.</span>Hostile reviews</h2>
        <p>
            The theory was subjected to two rounds of hostile review. The first found one valid objection,
            eleven partially valid, and zero invalid — a distribution that pointed at presentation, not at a
            wrong core. The second round raised twelve "FATAL" issues; each was re-evaluated against the
            executable tests.
        </p>
        <p>
            What changed was framing, not physics: "closed" became "dispositioned", "no-go" became "conditional
            no-go", and "derivation" became "ontological reinterpretation". The tally — 6 resolved, 6 partially
            resolved, 0 open — is a record of overstatements downgraded, not of results retracted.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">6.</span>TQM v1.0</h2>
        <p>
            The compressed endpoint is the theory described throughout this atlas: two irreducible primitives
            (the individuation principle Q and genuine randomness) plus one contingent number (M²). The
            Schrödinger dynamical core is executable and tested; the Einstein recovery remains logical, not
            mathematical. The theory is ready as a foundation monograph and a research-program paper — and
            honestly not yet as a derivation paper.
        </p>

